<ContentLayout
  title="Content Audit Services"
  description="Done-for-you content audit services identifying thin, duplicate, and underperforming pages, with a keep, update, consolidate, or remove decision for every page. Pricing from $297."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  schema={schema}
  showLeadForm={true}
  publishDate="2026-08-26"
>

<p>Content audit services deliver the same analysis our <a href="/website-content-audit/">content audit guide</a> teaches you to do yourself. An analyst runs it against your actual site. Every page gets a keep, update, consolidate, or remove decision instead of just a list of flagged issues.</p>

<h2 id="whats-included">What's Included</h2>

<p><strong>Full content inventory</strong> — every indexed page catalogued with word count and traffic pulled from Search Console. A full site crawl confirms what's actually live.</p>

<p><strong>Quality scoring</strong> — each page scored against thinness, duplication (internal and cross-domain), and topical relevance to your core business.</p>

<p><strong>Competitive gap analysis</strong> — topics your competitors rank for that you have no content covering. Each gap is ranked by opportunity size.</p>

<p><strong>Action plan</strong> — a prioritised list marking every page as keep-as-is, update (with what to change), consolidate (with the merge target), or remove. The order follows expected traffic impact.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-hire-this-out">When to Hire This Out</h2>

<p>Sites with more than a few hundred pages usually benefit most because manual review at that scale takes days a small team doesn't have. Sites planning a redesign or CMS migration benefit from an audit beforehand — migrating thin or duplicate content just carries the problem to the new platform. Sites with a traffic plateau despite regular publishing often have a content-quality problem an audit surfaces quickly.</p>

